--TEST--
an instance method reached through its class name from a frame with no `$this` is refused, while `self::` inside an instance method still forwards that frame's own — `rule:statements/static-is-a-member-modifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:statements/static-is-a-member-modifier` keeps PHP's semantics for `static` unchanged: a method without
// it is entered through a value, and a method with it is entered through the
// class. `nvs_ir::lower::expr` reads the enclosing frame's `$this` for a
// non-static target, so the refusal below is what stands between that read and
// a panic.
class Ledger {
    public function total(): int {
        // Legal, and the reason the refusal asks about the frame rather than
        // about the spelling: `self::` inside an instance method forwards this
        // frame's own `$this` to a non-static target.
        return self::rows();
    }

    private function rows(): int {
        return 2;
    }

    public static function zero(): int {
        return 0;
    }
}

// A static method through the class name is the ordinary spelling and is not
// refused — one error below, not two.
echo Ledger::zero(), "\n";
echo Ledger::total(), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0778]: `Ledger::total()` is not `static`, so it is called on a value
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
